file construction/REDS.md books %806 -- the dangling else the hot pass found, measured both ways and left to the lane that owns ryekey
obs The hot roster pass found a red that is not mine and booked it as %806. tools/r/ryekey_witness.rish:55 writes if (cond) then assert X else "msg", and Rishi binds that else to the if, so the false branch executes a bare string and refuses as UnknownStatement. Proven both ways in a pen: bound to a control emitting ryekey_verdict=green the line walks free, bound to one emitting machine_fact it refuses. The guard therefore reds exactly where its own control declares nothing is wrong.
obs Two of the four hot-pass reds are machine facts this lap did not make and did not close: built_tool_freshness reads rye 88,600 seconds behind rye/src/main.rye, and ryekey's control names the same staleness. remember_git_nib clears on this send.
obs The send met a moved upstream, nineteen commits including a fold of two ledger rows, so the row was rebased and re-derived from the anointed spine at 806 rather than the 803 this tree had read alone. reds_pin_capacity read over_bound_foldable before the rebase and ok with 912 bytes of headroom after -- which is derived-spine rule 2 doing exactly its job.
scope one claim opened and pushed before the build; one instrument, one control, one witness, one paper; no peer's file touched
status GREEN -- page_evict witness GREEN with control cases=11 fail=0, tame_style_check GREEN, width-check GREEN, paper A/91, three code artifacts B or better
recommend keep-going -- the tar reading wants its NVMe control and its fio latency run, and both are one lap each
